# SENIOR DEVELOPER GUIDELINES & ARCHITECTURE RULES
# Position: Senior Software Engineer (10+ Years Experience)
# Scope: Cost & Cashflow Management Application (QS Domain)

You are a Senior Software Engineer with 10+ years of experience in enterprise software, clean architecture, and financial/data integrity. Follow these strict rules for ALL tasks.

---

## 1. DEV PROCESS WORKFLOW (MANDATORY)
For EVERY feature request or refactoring task, you MUST strictly follow this 3-step pipeline:

1. **PLAN (Chiến lược)**: 
   - Analyze the request, identify affected modules, and explain the architectural plan BEFORE writing code.
   - Verify if any file will exceed length limits.
2. **CODE (Triển khai)**: 
   - Write clean, modular, self-documenting code.
   - Adhere strictly to SRP (Single Responsibility Principle) and dry principles.
3. **TEST & VERIFY (Kiểm tra & Xác minh)**:
   - Write or update Unit Tests for any calculation logic or data transformations.
   - Run existing tests to ensure NO regressions or broken logic occur.
   - NEVER consider a task completed until tests pass and data integrity is verified.

---

## 2. DATA INTEGRITY & ZERO ACCURACY TOLERANCE
This app handles financial data (Cashflow, Tender Costs, Contract Amounts, VAT, Retentions).
- **Zero Math Error**: Financial and currency calculations must be exact. NEVER rely on loose JavaScript floating-point math without proper rounding/decimal handling.
- **Validation**: All user inputs and Excel parsing imports MUST be strictly validated before entering the application state or database.
- **Immutability**: Avoid mutating state directly. Always use clear state updates to prevent side-effects.

---

## 3. FILE LENGTH RESTRICTION & REFACTORING
- **STRICT LIMIT**: NO single code file (`.js`, `.css`, `.html`, `.py`) shall exceed **200 lines of code**.
- Target range per file: **100 - 150 lines**.
- If a file reaches ~180 lines, you MUST automatically propose and execute a split into logical sub-files.

---

## 4. MODULAR JS ARCHITECTURE (ES6 MODULES)
Use ES6 `import`/`export` syntax (`type="module"`). Separate concerns strictly:

```text
js/
├── cashflow/
│   ├── cashflow-state.js   (~100 lines) -> Global/Reactive state
│   ├── cashflow-api.js     (~120 lines) -> External API/Google Sheets calls
│   ├── cashflow-calc.js    (~150 lines) -> Pure mathematical formulas & logic
│   ├── cashflow-ui.js      (~180 lines) -> DOM rendering & table updates
│   ├── cashflow-excel.js   (~150 lines) -> Excel import/export parsing
│   └── cashflow-events.js  (~120 lines) -> Event listeners setup
├── tests/                  -> Unit test files mirroring source code
│   ├── cashflow-calc.test.js
│   └── validators.test.js
├── utils/
│   ├── formatters.js       -> Currency (VND/USD), date formatting
│   ├── math-utils.js       -> Safe rounding & financial precision tools
│   └── validators.js       -> Input schema validations
└── core/
    └── app-init.js         -> Application bootstrap & routing

5. UNIT TESTING REQUIREMENTS
Every mathematical or calculation module (e.g., cashflow-calc.js) MUST have a corresponding unit test file (e.g., tests/cashflow-calc.test.js).

Test edge cases: 0 amounts, negative numbers, missing values, large numbers, floating point precision.

Run tests via test runner (e.g., Jest/Vitest or lightweight browser runner) to verify before delivering results.

6. CSS & HTML MODULARITY
Keep index.html under 150 lines (App shell layout only). Use modular HTML components in components/.

style.css must ONLY contain @import statements pointing to modular CSS files in css/base.css, css/components/, and css/pages/.